# Knowledge review rules KB01–KB18

Use this maintained English guidance for sections 1–4 of the [frozen Chinese
KB rules](KB规则.md). The [provenance](KB规则.provenance.json) records the original
`知识库设计/知识库自检规则.md`, source digest, and selected sections. Use the frozen
source for original quotations and machine catalog bindings. Apply the
[shared conventions](共同约定.md), effective project decisions, and actual
permissions. The [knowledge governance process](knowledge-governance-process.md)
explains S01–S11. Use the [evaluation and acceptance cases](knowledge-evaluation-and-cases.md)
to plan separate runtime verification.

## 1. Scope and supported conclusions

Report which currently permitted sources support a claim. Verify factual
accuracy, adoption, requirements, design, permissions, and stage confirmation
independently. Treat indexing, search rank, adoption, and model agreement as
records of those events or judgments.

- Keep documents and derived indexes local in the historical design. Its scope covers documents and excludes code files and source repository analysis.
- Share eligible reference rules and isolate project originals and derivatives. Cross-project use requires explicit selection of the source project, material, and purpose and a valid grant record.
- A manual import can include explicit adoption of the selected version, scope, and use. Retain all other material as candidates. Use a real operator decision for adoption and keep system searches and model recommendations in candidate review.
- Send only task-relevant excerpts and citations to a configured model API within current permission. Verify API configuration, adoption, cross-project grants, and transfer authority separately.
- Preserve factual support, adoption, stage confirmation, knowledge checks, and actual application acceptance as distinct states.

| Object | Supported conclusion | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Design document | The rules and process are written and available for review. | Establish implementation and application passes separately. |
| L1 structural check | Fields, IDs, relationships, and formats conform in the declared scope. | Verify citation meaning and runtime isolation separately. |
| L2 semantic review | The specified version's claims, citations, permissions, and conflict handling meet the declared conditions. | Verify retrieval quality, actual API transfers, and external facts separately. |
| L3 actual trial | Behavior was observed on the identified version, environment, and test data. | Limit the result to the exercised environment, formats, and assertions. |
| Adoption | The operator permits use of the selected candidate for the declared purpose. | Verify claims, formal requirements, and stage confirmation separately. |
| Stage confirmation | The user accepted the specified version and scope. | Establish application acceptance and external facts from their own evidence. |

## 2. Records and judgments

Pin the round, project and knowledge scope, object and rule versions, material
snapshot, effective decisions, retrieval, segmentation and model configuration
versions, applicability, and required levels. For each rule, object, and
required-level combination, record `satisfied`, `unsatisfied`, `unverified`, or
`not_applicable` with a reason.

Missing material or environment, unimplemented behavior, unexecuted work,
insufficient budget, and difficulty checking remain unverified or unsatisfied.
Use `not_applicable` only for an object or capability genuinely outside the
declared scope with a reviewable reason. Document review supports design
conclusions. Establish the L3 requirements with actual execution evidence.

Record at least `check_run_id`, `rule_id`, `object_id`, `object_version`,
`scope/project_id`, `required_levels`, `applicability_reason`, each level's
result and evidence locator, findings, affected objects, blocked scope, next
action, recheck conditions, and actual actor and time. For actual evidence,
also record environment, build and configuration, input snapshot, operations,
original output or controlled log locator, and content identity. Store examples
as examples.

Use the existing issue lifecycle from pending through verification, user
decision, environment block, or revision, then recheck and closure. Preserve
open status after pausing, cancelling, organizing the material, or adding a
test plan. Close only with current-version evidence, recheck of affected
objects, and applicable valid confirmation. Reopen on new counterexamples or
source changes and preserve the history.

Propagate blockers along actual dependencies. Identify the unusable source,
excerpt, citation, output, or action and link affected requirements, SOP
sections, checks, and tasks. Record unknown impact as an analysis gap. Continue
independent authorized work. Keep permission gaps, possible unauthorized
transfers, and significant data loss visible at any score.

## 3. Knowledge rules

The L3 column gives evidence required for future application checks. The
historical design recorded all such checks as unexecuted. L1 and L2 evidence
can start in the design and still requires actual objects and judgments. Fix
the required levels before review and retain applicable failures in scope.

